Z||000000||Matthew_Fiero||10-06-2001||01:24 AM||||i will be installing new lock cylinders for both doors on my 85 GT. The door just has power windows. how hard is it to take off the door panel, install the cylinders and replace the door panel? what are the steps? anything to be careful of?<BR>sorry for all the questions, trying to learn  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||10-06-2001||08:54 AM||||Try a search.  If you can't come up with anything, feel free to email me and I'd be happy to walk you through it.  It's not a difficult job.  The hardest part is trying to get the damn (yes, I cussed) C clips back on the new lock cylinders.  Other than that, it's a piece of cake.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Banzai||10-06-2001||09:38 AM||||I was just in Ogres Cave(top of page) last night. There is some excellent info in there on getting the skin off as well as alot of other info.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000003||SKIDMARK||10-06-2001||10:32 AM||||I had new locks made by a local GM. dealer.  I took the old locks to the dealer and they built me new ones to match the keys I already had. One key does the trunk and doors now!<BR>You can do the entire job by removeing the inner door panel. Like stated above, the tricky part is getting the clips back in place. Took me about and hour and a half. If you have never taken an inner door panel off, I suggest you take your time and don't force anything. Good luck.<p>[This message has been edited by SKIDMARK (edited 10-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||David Bartlett||10-07-2001||02:39 PM||||I got a Clifford Aero 3 with door locks installed in my car for $400 Cdn.. It saved me the time! They also wired up the windows to work by remote.||||reg||1
